                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Transformations**\n\nI'm currently focused on how input grids with blue shapes (1s) are altered, particularly with the introduction of red elements (2s). Example 1 highlights a 3x3 hollow square which is the first shape being looked at. My attention is now on figuring out the rules behind these transformations, especially whether red shapes are purely replacements or if they involve additions like crosses.\n\n\n**Identifying Transformation Rules**\n\nI've discovered a key pattern: hollow shapes are being replaced by crosses of '2's. The location of the cross seems critical. For the 3x3 hollow square, the '2' cross is centered at the hole. The 5x5 rectangle is replaced by a cross that spans the entire shape, centered at its center. I need to resolve why in a 4x4 shape the cross isn't centered at the center point.\n\n\n**Calculating Centroid Placement**\n\nI've been calculating how the center point is derived for placing the '2' cross within the hollow shapes. It appears to involve calculating the midpoints using integer division and the shape's bounds. For the 4x4 shape, row 2 and column 2 are indeed identified correctly. It seems the rule works regardless of size, even in Example 2.\n\n\n",
