                "text": "**Analyzing Shape Composition**\n\nI'm currently breaking down the given example to identify and characterize the connected components, or 'shapes,' formed by the 1s. So far, I've identified a hollow 3x3 square and a cross-shaped figure. The goal is to develop an understanding of how these basic shapes can be recognized.\n\n\n**Observing Transformations**\n\nI've been analyzing the transformations from the input to the output examples. The 5x5 hollow square is consistently converted into a cross of 2s centered within the bounding box. The other smaller shapes, like the 'L' shape, remain unchanged. Also, a 4x4 hollow square is turned to a cross of 2s, but the cross is slightly offset from the 5x5 square's position.\n\n\n**Detecting Shape Conversion**\n\nI've made a key discovery: any hollow square or rectangle consistently transforms into a cross of 2s! The centering of this cross is always at the center of the original shape. I'm now testing this hypothesis, and looking for more examples to see if I can find any exceptions.\n\n\n",
